{"metadata": {"file_suffix": "pdf", "producer": {"name": "mineru", "version": "4.0.7"}, "document": {"page_count": 10, "page_count_kind": "physical"}}, "extensions": {"mineru": {"tier": "standard", "parse_mode": "txt"}, "docvortex_layout": {"version": 1, "pages": [{"page_idx": 0, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}, {"page_idx": 1, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}, {"page_idx": 2, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}, {"page_idx": 3, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}, {"page_idx": 4, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}, {"page_idx": 5, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}, {"page_idx": 6, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}, {"page_idx": 7, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}, {"page_idx": 8, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}, {"page_idx": 9, "width_pt": 595.2760009765625, "height_pt": 841.8900146484375}]}}, "pages": [{"page_idx": 0, "blocks": [{"type": "doc_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.088, 0.204, 0.714, 0.24], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Understanding Attention"}], "level": 1}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.085, 0.261, 0.596, 0.282], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "From Q, K, V to Modern Transformer Attention"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.083, 0.345, 0.914, 0.379], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A technical handbook for understanding what attention computes, why the equation has its particular form, and how modern LLM systems execute it."}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.086, 0.923, 0.195, 0.94], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 1, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.086, 0.061, 0.45, 0.08], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "What this handbook promises"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.086, 0.097, 0.528, 0.112], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "By the end of this handbook, you should be able to look at"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.343, 0.121, 0.656, 0.154], "content": "\\text {Attention} (Q, K, V) = \\text {softmax} \\left(\\frac {Q K ^ {\\top}}{\\sqrt {d _ {k}}}\\right) V"}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.085, 0.164, 0.914, 0.209], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "and explain every object and operation in it: where Q, K, and V come from; what each element of "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "Q K ^ { \\top }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " means; why the scores are scaled; how masks change the allowed connections; why softmax is applied row-wise; and why multiplying by V produces a new representation for every query position."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.085, 0.215, 0.913, 0.257], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "You should also be able to explain how the same core mechanism appears in self-attention, cross-attention, causal language models, multi-head attention, KV-cached decoding, MQA, GQA, MLA, FlashAttention, and sparse/local attention."}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.086, 0.276, 0.203, 0.291], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Prerequisites"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.084, 0.302, 0.912, 0.332], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "You need basic comfort with vectors, matrices, matrix multiplication, dot products, softmax, and neural-network linear layers. No prior knowledge of Transformer internals is assumed."}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.086, 0.349, 0.143, 0.364], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Scope"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.085, 0.375, 0.913, 0.42], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "This is a handbook about attention. It touches RoPE, KV caching, MQA/GQA/MLA, and FlashAttention only to explain how they modify, support, or execute attention. Full inference serving, PagedAttention, long-context training, and model parallelism deserve separate handbooks."}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.087, 0.437, 0.211, 0.452], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Accuracy rule"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.084, 0.463, 0.913, 0.507], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Named methods are grounded in their primary papers; foundational explanations are cross-checked against current Stanford course material; implementation behavior is checked against current PyTorch documentation. Claims about historical papers are kept within what those papers actually establish. [1, 2, 3, 15, 16, 17]"}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 11, "bbox": [0.087, 0.961, 0.172, 0.974], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 12, "bbox": [0.899, 0.962, 0.912, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 2, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.087, 0.06, 0.2, 0.08], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Contents"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "index", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.085, 0.097, 0.914, 0.838], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "What this handbook promises 2   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "1 Before the Transformer: why attention appeared 4   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2 Attention as content-dependent weighted aggregation 5   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3 Notation and tensor shapes 6   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "4 Where Q, K, and V come from 7   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "5 The score matrix: "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "QK^{\\top}"}, {"type": "text", "content": " 8   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "6 Why divide by "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\sqrt{d_k}"}, {"type": "text", "content": "? 9   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "7 Masks: which connections are allowed? 10   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "8 Softmax and value aggregation 11   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "9 Worked example I: Q, K, V and raw scores 12   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "10 Worked example II: softmax weights 13   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "11 Worked example III: the output vectors 14   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "12 Self-attention vs cross-attention 15   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "13 Multi-head attention 16   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "14 Position: attention needs an ordering signal 17   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "15 Causal attention in an autoregressive language model 18   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "16 Training vs decoding 19   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "17 The KV cache 20   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "18 MHA, MQA, and GQA 21   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "19 MLA: compressing the KV representation 22   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "20 What exactly is quadratic? 23   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "21 FlashAttention: same attention, different execution 24   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "22 FlashAttention generations: optimize the bottleneck you actually have 25   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "23 Sparse and local attention 26   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "24 A transparent PyTorch implementation 27   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "25 Production SDPA in PyTorch 28   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "26 Common implementation mistakes 29   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "27 Common misconceptions 30   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "28 Are attention weights explanations? 31   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "29 The complete mental model 32   "}]}, {"type": "text", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "References and further reading 33"}]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.088, 0.962, 0.172, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.9, 0.962, 0.911, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 3, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.086, 0.061, 0.706, 0.08], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "1 Before the Transformer: why attention appeared"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.084, 0.097, 0.913, 0.17], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Early neural sequence-to-sequence systems often encoded an entire source sequence into a single fixed-length vector and asked the decoder to generate the target sequence from that representation. Bahdanau, Cho, and Bengio argued that this fixed-vector interface could become a bottleneck, especially as source sequences grew longer. Their model allowed the decoder to form a different context vector at each output step by softly weighting encoder states according to their relevance to the current decoding state. [1]"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.085, 0.175, 0.912, 0.233], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The important idea was not yet “self-attention.” The decoder was using a learned compatibility mechanism to decide which source representations should contribute more strongly to the current output. Luong, Pham, and Manning later studied multiple NMT attention variants, including global attention over all source positions and local attention over a subset. [2]"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.085, 0.239, 0.913, 0.298], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The 2017 Transformer changed the role of attention much more radically. Instead of using recurrence or convolution as the main mechanism for moving information through a sequence, it built its encoder and decoder around attention plus position-wise feed-forward layers. The paper describes self-attention as relating different positions of a single sequence to compute new representations. [3]"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.085, 0.313, 0.914, 0.358], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Mental model.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " Attention did not begin as “the Transformer equation.” The durable idea is learned, contentdependent aggregation: a query determines how strongly different key-value pairs contribute to an output. The Transformer made a particular attention formulation the central sequence-mixing operation."}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.086, 0.38, 0.258, 0.395], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "What we will study"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.085, 0.406, 0.913, 0.451], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The rest of the handbook focuses on "}, {"type": "text", "content": "scaled dot-product attention", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": ", because that is the form used by the Transformer and by modern LLM attention kernels. Earlier additive-attention mechanisms are historically important, but their scoring function differs."}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.087, 0.961, 0.172, 0.974], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.9, 0.962, 0.911, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "4"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 4, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.086, 0.061, 0.761, 0.081], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2 Attention as content-dependent weighted aggregation"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.085, 0.097, 0.912, 0.141], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "An attention function can be described as mapping a "}, {"type": "text", "content": "query", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " and a set of "}, {"type": "text", "content": "key-value pairs", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " to an output. Each key is used to determine how compatible its corresponding value is with the query; the output is a weighted combination of the values. This general description appears directly in the Transformer paper. [3]"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.086, 0.147, 0.794, 0.162], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "For one query vector "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "q"}, {"type": "text", "content": " and key-value pairs "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "( k _ { j } , v _ { j } )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " , scaled dot-product attention can be written"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.322, 0.172, 0.675, 0.209], "content": "s _ {j} = \\frac {q ^ {\\top} k _ {j}}{\\sqrt {d _ {k}}}, \\quad a _ {j} = \\frac {e ^ {s _ {j}}}{\\sum_ {r} e ^ {s _ {r}}}, \\quad o = \\sum_ {j} a _ {j} v _ {j}."}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.085, 0.218, 0.912, 0.263], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The score "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "s _ { j }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " is a learned compatibility signal because "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "q"}, {"type": "text", "content": " and "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "k _ { j }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " themselves come from learned projections. Softmax turns the scores into nonnegative weights that sum to one across the allowed keys. The output o is then a weighted average in value space."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.085, 0.268, 0.913, 0.313], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "This language is more precise than common personifications such as “the query asks a question and the key contains the answer.” Those analogies can be memorable, but they are not the mechanism. A query and key are vectors in learned representation spaces. Their dot product supplies a scalar compatibility score."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.085, 0.328, 0.912, 0.358], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Mental model.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " The key chooses the weight; the value supplies the content being mixed. That separation is the conceptual reason attention uses both K and V ."}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.086, 0.381, 0.264, 0.396], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A useful abstraction"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.085, 0.407, 0.578, 0.422], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "For many queries at once, attention becomes matrix computation:"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.306, 0.432, 0.692, 0.45], "content": "S = Q K ^ {\\top}, \\qquad A = \\operatorname{softmax} (S / \\sqrt {d _ {k}}), \\qquad O = A V."}, {"type": "text", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.085, 0.461, 0.458, 0.475], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The next pages unpack each object and its shape."}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 11, "bbox": [0.087, 0.961, 0.172, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 12, "bbox": [0.899, 0.962, 0.912, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "5"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 5, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.086, 0.061, 0.452, 0.08], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3 Notation and tensor shapes"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.086, 0.097, 0.119, 0.11], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Let"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.093, 0.121, 0.235, 0.136], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "• B = batch size,"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.094, 0.145, 0.352, 0.159], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "• L = number of query positions,"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.094, 0.166, 0.381, 0.182], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "• S = number of key/value positions,"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.094, 0.189, 0.339, 0.205], "content": [{"type": "equation_inline", "content": "H _ { q }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " = number of query heads,"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.094, 0.212, 0.376, 0.227], "content": [{"type": "equation_inline", "content": "H _ { k v }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " = number of key/value heads,"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.094, 0.234, 0.389, 0.249], "content": [{"type": "equation_inline", "content": "d _ { h } ="}, {"type": "text", "content": " per-head query/key dimension,"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.094, 0.257, 0.352, 0.271], "content": [{"type": "equation_inline", "content": "d _ { v }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " = per-head value dimension."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.085, 0.281, 0.737, 0.297], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "For a single head and a single example, we can ignore batch/head dimensions and write"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.345, 0.305, 0.651, 0.322], "content": "Q \\in \\mathbb {R} ^ {L \\times d _ {h}}, \\quad K \\in \\mathbb {R} ^ {S \\times d _ {h}}, \\quad V \\in \\mathbb {R} ^ {S \\times d _ {v}}."}, {"type": "text", "index": 11, "bbox": [0.085, 0.333, 0.132, 0.348], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Then"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 12, "bbox": [0.316, 0.345, 0.68, 0.363], "content": "Q K ^ {\\top} \\in \\mathbb {R} ^ {L \\times S}, \\qquad A \\in \\mathbb {R} ^ {L \\times S}, \\qquad A V \\in \\mathbb {R} ^ {L \\times d _ {v}}."}, {"type": "text", "index": 13, "bbox": [0.085, 0.376, 0.235, 0.39], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Two details matter."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 14, "bbox": [0.085, 0.397, 0.914, 0.442], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "First, the attention matrix is not always square. In self-attention over a single full sequence, "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "L = S ."}, {"type": "text", "content": " In cross-attention, or in cached decoding where a short query block attends to a longer prefix, L and S can differ. Current PyTorch SDPA documentation explicitly uses separate query length L and source length S. [17]"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 15, "bbox": [0.085, 0.447, 0.913, 0.477], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Second, modern grouped attention can have "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "H _ { q } \\neq H _ { k v }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " . Standard MHA usually has the same number of query and KV heads; "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { M Q A }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " uses one KV head; GQA uses an intermediate number. [4, 9]"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 16, "bbox": [0.084, 0.487, 0.912, 0.517], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Important.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " Do not silently use "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "n \\times n"}, {"type": "text", "content": " as the universal shape of attention weights. It is correct for same-length self-attention, but it obscures cross-attention and incremental inference."}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 17, "bbox": [0.087, 0.961, 0.172, 0.974], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 18, "bbox": [0.899, 0.962, 0.912, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "6"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 6, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.086, 0.061, 0.495, 0.08], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "4 Where Q, K, and V come from"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.085, 0.097, 0.649, 0.112], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Suppose the input to an attention layer is a matrix of token representations"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.441, 0.121, 0.557, 0.136], "content": "X \\in \\mathbb {R} ^ {n \\times d _ {\\mathrm{model}}}."}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.085, 0.149, 0.569, 0.164], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A standard self-attention layer applies learned linear projections:"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.332, 0.174, 0.665, 0.19], "content": "Q = X W _ {Q}, \\qquad K = X W _ {K}, \\qquad V = X W _ {V}."}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.085, 0.201, 0.192, 0.215], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "For one head,"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.331, 0.213, 0.666, 0.23], "content": "W _ {Q}, W _ {K} \\in \\mathbb {R} ^ {d _ {\\mathrm{model}} \\times d _ {h}}, \\qquad W _ {V} \\in \\mathbb {R} ^ {d _ {\\mathrm{model}} \\times d _ {v}}."}, {"type": "text", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.084, 0.236, 0.913, 0.28], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "In multi-head attention, each head conceptually has different learned projections, or an implementation computes one larger projection and reshapes it into heads. The Transformer paper defines multi-head attention in terms of separate learned projections for each head. [3]"}]}, {"type": "image", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.332, 0.297, 0.667, 0.411], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.332, 0.297, 0.667, 0.411], "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.085, 0.432, 0.914, 0.463], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Q, K, and V are therefore not fixed semantic roles attached to tokens. They are learned views of the current hidden representations. A token can have different query/key/value vectors in different layers and different heads."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.086, 0.479, 0.911, 0.509], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Mental model.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " The model learns two spaces for deciding compatibility (Q and K) and a space for the information that will be aggregated (V )."}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 11, "bbox": [0.087, 0.961, 0.172, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 12, "bbox": [0.899, 0.962, 0.911, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "7"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 7, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.086, 0.06, 0.402, 0.08], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "5 The score matrix: "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "Q K ^ { \\top }"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.084, 0.097, 0.504, 0.112], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Take query row "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "q _ { i }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " and key row "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "k _ { j }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " . Their dot product is"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.453, 0.121, 0.545, 0.139], "content": "S _ {i j} = \\boldsymbol {q} _ {i} ^ {\\top} \\boldsymbol {k} _ {j}."}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.085, 0.148, 0.77, 0.165], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The complete matrix multiplication "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "Q K ^ { \\top }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " computes every query-key dot product in parallel:"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.359, 0.174, 0.637, 0.252], "content": "Q K ^ {\\top} = \\left[ \\begin{array}{c c c c} q _ {1} ^ {\\top} k _ {1} & q _ {1} ^ {\\top} k _ {2} & \\dots & q _ {1} ^ {\\top} k _ {S} \\\\ q _ {2} ^ {\\top} k _ {1} & q _ {2} ^ {\\top} k _ {2} & \\dots & q _ {2} ^ {\\top} k _ {S} \\\\ \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\ q _ {L} ^ {\\top} k _ {1} & q _ {L} ^ {\\top} k _ {2} & \\dots & q _ {L} ^ {\\top} k _ {S} \\end{array} \\right]."}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.085, 0.263, 0.775, 0.277], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Every row corresponds to one query position. Every column corresponds to one key position."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.084, 0.283, 0.911, 0.313], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The dot product is not a probability. It is an unnormalized compatibility score. It can be positive or negative and its magnitude depends on the scale and dimension of the vectors."}]}, {"type": "chart", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.313, 0.33, 0.686, 0.455], "content": [{"type": "chart_body", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.313, 0.33, 0.686, 0.455], "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.085, 0.481, 0.912, 0.524], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Important.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " Calling the score “similarity” is convenient but incomplete. Dot-product attention learns projections specifically so the resulting compatibility is useful for the task. It need not behave like a generic semantic-similarity metric."}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.087, 0.961, 0.172, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.899, 0.962, 0.912, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "8"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 8, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.086, 0.06, 0.365, 0.081], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "6 Why divide by "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\sqrt { d _ { k } } ?"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.084, 0.097, 0.586, 0.112], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The Transformer scales the query-key dot products before softmax:"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.454, 0.12, 0.544, 0.154], "content": "\\widetilde {S} = \\frac {Q K ^ {\\top}}{\\sqrt {d _ {k}}}."}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.085, 0.162, 0.912, 0.192], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The paper gives a specific motivation. Suppose the components of q and k are independent random variables with mean zero and variance one. Then"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.44, 0.188, 0.557, 0.227], "content": "q ^ {\\top} k = \\sum_ {r = 1} ^ {d _ {k}} q _ {r} k _ {r}"}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.084, 0.231, 0.914, 0.275], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "has mean zero and variance "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "d _ { k }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " . As "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "d _ { k }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " grows, unscaled dot products can therefore grow in magnitude. Largemagnitude logits can push softmax into a saturated regime with very small gradients. Dividing by "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\sqrt { d _ { k } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " normalizes this illustrative variance from "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "d _ { k }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " to approximately one. [3]"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.085, 0.281, 0.431, 0.296], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A short derivation under those assumptions is"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.38, 0.306, 0.619, 0.345], "content": "\\operatorname{Var} (q ^ {\\top} k) = \\sum_ {r = 1} ^ {d _ {k}} \\operatorname{Var} (q _ {r} k _ {r}) = d _ {k},"}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.086, 0.354, 0.109, 0.365], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "so"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.433, 0.361, 0.564, 0.395], "content": "\\operatorname{Var} \\left(\\frac {q ^ {\\top} k}{\\sqrt {d _ {k}}}\\right) \\approx 1."}, {"type": "text", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.085, 0.41, 0.912, 0.455], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Important.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " This derivation is a motivation, not a claim that learned query/key components in a trained Transformer are always independent, zero-mean, unit-variance random variables. The paper explicitly presents those assumptions as an illustration of why dot products can grow with dimension."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 11, "bbox": [0.086, 0.47, 0.912, 0.5], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Mental model.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " Scaling is about keeping score magnitudes in a range where softmax behaves usefully as dimensionality grows. It is not a normalization of Q or K into unit vectors."}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 12, "bbox": [0.087, 0.961, 0.172, 0.974], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 13, "bbox": [0.899, 0.962, 0.911, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "9"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 9, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.086, 0.061, 0.589, 0.08], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "7 Masks: which connections are allowed?"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.085, 0.097, 0.912, 0.127], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Before softmax, an attention implementation can add a mask or bias to the score matrix. For causal language modeling, future key positions must not influence an earlier query position. A common additive mask is"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.416, 0.136, 0.581, 0.174], "content": "M _ {i j} = \\left\\{ \\begin{array}{l l} 0, & j \\leq i, \\\\ - \\infty , & j > i. \\end{array} \\right."}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.085, 0.183, 0.131, 0.197], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Then"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.391, 0.193, 0.605, 0.226], "content": "A = \\operatorname{softmax} \\left(\\frac {Q K ^ {\\top}}{\\sqrt {d _ {k}}} + M\\right)."}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.085, 0.231, 0.62, 0.246], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Because "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "e ^ { - \\infty } = 0 ."}, {"type": "text", "content": " masked entries receive zero probability after softmax."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.085, 0.252, 0.597, 0.268], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "For a length-4 square causal attention matrix, the allowed pattern is"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.432, 0.276, 0.568, 0.351], "content": "\\left[ \\begin{array}{c c c c} \\checkmark & \\times & \\times & \\times \\\\ \\checkmark & \\checkmark & \\times & \\times \\\\ \\checkmark & \\checkmark & \\checkmark & \\times \\\\ \\checkmark & \\checkmark & \\checkmark & \\checkmark \\end{array} \\right]."}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.085, 0.36, 0.912, 0.39], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The original Transformer decoder implements autoregressive masking by setting illegal pre-softmax connections to −∞. [3]"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.085, 0.395, 0.912, 0.44], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Masks are broader than causality. Padding masks can prevent attention to padding tokens; structured attention can restrict connectivity to local windows, blocks, or other patterns. Current PyTorch SDPA accepts Boolean or additive floating-point masks and separately supports a causal mode. [17]"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.085, 0.45, 0.912, 0.494], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Important.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " In non-square attention "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "( L \\neq S )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " , “lower triangular” requires an alignment convention. Current PyTorch documentation explicitly distinguishes square and non-square causal bias behavior. Do not blindly construct an "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "L \\times S"}, {"type": "text", "content": " triangle without checking what the API expects."}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 11, "bbox": [0.087, 0.961, 0.172, 0.974], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "@techNmak"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 12, "bbox": [0.893, 0.962, 0.912, 0.973], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "10"}]}]}], "is_full_document": true, "schema": "docvortex.middle", "schema_version": "2.0"}